## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical Azure storage patterns, including idempotency design, per-tenant isolation, and integration with core Azure services—essential skills for building enterprise-ready serverless applications.

## Key Concepts
- Azure Functions HTTP trigger for API endpoints
- Azure Table Storage partitioning strategy using tenantId as PartitionKey and fileName as RowKey for efficient, tenant-isolated queries
- ADLS Gen2 Blob Storage for scalable file persistence
- Idempotent design patterns using conditional inserts or upserts in Table Storage
- Retriable operations through transactional writes or try-catch with appropriate retry logic

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton: HTTP-triggered function
module.exports = async function(context, req) {
  const { tenantId, fileName, contentBase64 } = req.body;
  const tableClient = /* Table Storage client */;
  const blobClient = /* ADLS Gen2 blob client */;

  const existing = await tableClient.getEntity(tenantId, fileName);
  if (existing) {
    return { status: 200, body: { message: 'File already uploaded', ...existing } };
  }

  const content = Buffer.from(contentBase64, 'base64');
  await blobClient.upload(content);
  await tableClient.upsert({ partitionKey: tenantId, rowKey: fileName, ... });

  return { status: 201, body: { message: 'File uploaded successfully' } };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle large files (streaming) to avoid memory pressure?
- How would you test idempotency guarantees with concurrent uploads?
- How would you implement retry logic for transient failures?
- What security considerations apply to per-tenant file access?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests crypto maturity, per-tenant isolation, and how encryption keys impact storage systems. It also probes end-to-end tracing, secure key management, and real-world rotation workflows in Azure.

## Key Concepts
- Envelope encryption with per-tenant DEK
- KEK and DEK lifecycle in Azure Key Vault
- Zero-downtime KEK rotation using Durable Functions
- End-to-end tracing across Functions, Cosmos DB, and Data Lake
- Per-env CI/CD and access governance

## Code Example
```javascript
import { DefaultAzureCredential } from "@azure/identity";
import { KeyClient, CryptographyClient } from "@azure/keyvault-keys";

const credential = new DefaultAzureCredential();
const keyClient = new KeyClient("https://<vault>.vault.azure.net", credential);
async function fetchTenantDEK(tenantId) {
  const key = await keyClient.getKey(`dek-${tenantId}`);
  // In practice, wrap/unwrap with a CryptographyClient here
  return key;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate zero-downtime rotation impact on latency?
- How would you audit key access and revoke keys per tenant if needed?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design tenant-isolated, low-latency inference with real-time routing, drift monitoring, and safe rollbacks in a mixed Azure stack.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant isolation and routing
- Canary deployments and canary traffic shaping
- Real-time feature storage and model management
- Drift detection and rollback triggers
- Observability and latency budgeting

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: choose model version per tenant and route inference
function routeInference(tenantId, event) {
  const modelVersion = selectVersionForTenant(tenantId);
  const embedding = redis.get(` Embedding:${tenantId}`);
  const score = mlService.infer(modelVersion, embedding, event);
  return score;
}
```

